      "study_summary_A": {
        "approach": "The research design involves constructing an experimental LiDAR setup combining dynamic and static optical components. 1) **Hardware Integration**: A pulsed laser source is directed into an Acousto-Optic Deflector (AOD) for fast, small-angle steering. The deflected beam is then focused onto a custom-fabricated Effective Refractive Index (ERI) metasurface. The metasurface has a radially symmetric phase profile designed to amplify the incident angle linearly. 2) **Imaging Modes**: Two configurations were tested: a standard single-beam mode for wide-angle scanning and a dual-beam mode utilizing both the zero-order (undiffracted) and first-order (diffracted) light to simultaneously capture high-resolution central images and low-resolution peripheral images. 3) **Validation**: The system was validated through 1D/2D ranging of static objects, 3D line scanning of a room, and time-series imaging of a rotating chopper wheel to measure dynamic event tracking at high frame rates (up to MHz).",
        "contribution_and_limits": [
          {
            "contribution": "Proposes and experimentally validates a hybrid AOD-metasurface architecture that decouples scanning speed from FoV constraints, achieving MHz speeds over a 150° FoV.",
            "limits": "The demonstration uses a visible wavelength (633 nm) and low power (~10 mW), limiting outdoor/long-range applicability. Depth resolution is coarse (Δz ≈ 5 cm) due to detector rise time limits. Data processing latency prevents real-time video output at full acquisition speeds without FPGA/ASIC support."
          },
          {
            "contribution": "Demonstrates 'multizone' imaging by exploiting diffraction orders, providing a hardware-based solution for simultaneous central/peripheral vision without computational fusion.",
            "limits": "The efficiency of the first-order deflection is moderate (~40%), leading to significant optical loss. The zero-order beam remains undeflected, requiring careful spatial filtering to separate zones."
          },
          {
            "contribution": "Provides a scalable fabrication route using nanoimprint lithography (NIL) for large-area metasurfaces, addressing manufacturing complexity compared to OPAs.",
            "limits": "The metasurface is passive and fixed after fabrication; it cannot dynamically correct aberrations or change its focal properties. Performance is specific to the design wavelength and incident angle range."
          }
        ],
        "key_findings": [
          {
            "conditions": "Single-axis scanning with AOD + ERI Metasurface cascade; λ=633nm.",
            "finding": "The system achieved a total Field of View of 150° × 150°, expanding the ~2° FoV of the AOD. The deflection angle showed linear dependence on the AOD voltage within small angles."
          },
          {
            "conditions": "Dual-detector scheme separating zero-order and first-order beams.",
            "finding": "Simultaneous multi-zone imaging was demonstrated: a high-resolution, narrow FoV (central) image and a low-resolution, wide FoV (peripheral) image were acquired concurrently, mimicking human vision."
          },
          {
            "conditions": "Dynamic imaging of a rotating chopper wheel (nominal 100 Hz).",
            "finding": "The system tracked the rotation at frame rates of 741, 1020, and 3401 fps, recovering an average speed of 92.71 Hz. It also detected mechanical imperfections (wobbling) in the wheel."
