        "paper_kind": "empirical",
        "problem_or_question": "Existing LiDAR technologies struggle to simultaneously achieve a large Field of View (FoV) and high frame rates (kHz/MHz). Mechanical scanners are slow; MEMS have limited FoV; Optical Phased Arrays (OPA) are complex to manufacture. There is a need for a scalable, high-speed scanning solution that can provide both wide coverage and high resolution for applications like ADAS.",
        "research_scope": "LiDAR beam steering technology; specifically, the integration of acousto-optic deflectors (AOD) with passive effective refractive index (ERI) metasurfaces to achieve wide-field-of-view (FoV) and high-speed scanning.",
        "work_summary": "This study addresses the trade-off between field of view and scanning speed in LiDAR systems. Commercial LiDARs using mechanical or MEMS scanners are limited by low frame rates or narrow FoV, while optical phased arrays offer speed but face manufacturing challenges. The authors propose a hybrid approach: cascading a commercial AOD, which provides ultrafast but narrow (~2°) beam deflection, with a large-area passive ERI metasurface. The metasurface acts as a static angular amplifier, converting the small input angle variations from the AOD into a much larger output scan range (up to 150°). Using a 633 nm laser source and time-of-flight (ToF) detection, the system demonstrates 2D and 3D imaging capabilities. It achieves MHz-level deflection speeds and enables simultaneous multi-zone imaging (mimicking human peripheral and foveal vision) by separating zero-order and first-order diffraction beams. The work serves as a proof-of-concept for high-speed, wide-angle LiDAR suitable for autonomous driving and robotics."
